[90.211061, "o", "  ⚙ note \r\n    ✓ note — Broken-pip diagnosis pattern that worked: `python3 -m pip` s\r\nFixed. Your pip install had been **hollowed out**, not misconfigured.\r\n\r\n## What was actually wrong\r\n\r\n`python3 -m pip` failed with `No module named pip`, yet `/usr/local/bin/pip` still existed and was executable. That mismatch was the clue: someone/something deleted the `pip/` package directory from `site-packages` but left the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` metadata behind. So pip's launcher scripts still ran and imported `pip._internal.cli.main` — straight into a directory that wasn't there.\r\n\r\nThe numbers made it precise: `RECORD` (pip's own manifest) listed **841** files under `pip/`; **0** existed on disk.\r\n\r\n## Why I could fix it with no network\r\n\r\nThis session has `network = false` in `.gnomon/policy.toml`, so `get-pip.py` and `pip install pip` were both unavailable. But CPython ships a copy of pip inside `ensurepip/_bundled/` — a wheel that survives exactly this kind of damage. Before trusting it I verified it wa"]
[90.211167, "o", "s the right one:\r\n\r\n- All **430** `.py` files in the wheel hash-matched `RECORD` exactly (0 mismatches), so I was restoring genuine bytes, not a doctored or version-skewed copy.\r\n- The 841-vs-430 gap was entirely `__pycache__` entries, which `RECORD` lists but wheels don't contain — normal, not corruption.\r\n\r\nThen I unzipped the wheel into `site-packages` and re-verified: **0 missing files, 0 hash mismatches** against `RECORD`.\r\n\r\n## Proof it works\r\n\r\n- `python3 -m pip --version`, `pip`, `pip3` → all report `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`\r\n- A real end-to-end install: I built a throwaway wheel and ran `pip install --no-index --no-deps --target=...`, which got `Successfully installed smoketest-1.0` and the installed package then imported and ran. This exercises pip's whole install machinery without needing the internet.\r\n- `pip list`, `pip download --help`, and `python3 -m venv` (which seeds its own pip) all succeed.\r\n\r\nTemp artifacts cleaned up; nothing in `/app"]
[90.211215, "o", "` was modified (it isn't a git repo).\r\n\r\n## Two things to flag\r\n\r\n**I could not test a real PyPI download**, because network is off by policy. Install-from-index is the one path left unverified. The failure mode you'd still see is a *network/TLS/proxy* problem, which is unrelated to what was broken here — if that's what you originally hit, rerun with `/network on` and I'll diagnose it.\r\n\r\n**Worth asking how this happened.** Deleting a package's code while leaving its metadata is an unusual failure — a killed `pip uninstall`, a partial container layer, or a `rm -rf` gone wrong. `site-packages` here contains only pip and a stray `README.txt`, which suggests a deliberately slimmed image. If other Python installs on this machine share that image, check them the same way:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\npython3 -m pip --version\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf you want pip newer than 24.3.1, that needs network: `python3 -m pip install -U pip`.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-flash-next tools=16 surface=0192700a1561\r\n"]
